running is an activity fundamentally  based on breath  you run as far as your lungs will take  you  if running long distance you drink in an  ocean of air  trying to extract enough oxygen every  second to keep going  for some people however just getting  enough oxygen in their daily lives  can be a real problem cystic fibrosis is  a  multi-organ genetic condition that  affects  over 10 000 people in the uk people  think of it usually as a lung condition  but it also affects um pretty much um  everything in the body  in particular the digestive system so  it's caused by an error  in a gene and i forget which one which  impacts the ability of the body to to  control the salt transfer through cell  walls  and this means that there is if in the  lungs for example this  means that the mucus within the lungs  kind of  dries out and becomes much thicker and  much stickier  and the body's normal use for mucus is  obviously capture  any kind of bacteria or particulates if  they get that low  and naturally expel them because of  how the mucus is that doesn't happen and  you end up with a  buildup of uh infections essentially  which over time cause lung damage and  scarring  members of my discord community or  viewers of my twitch channel will  recognize dan hanvey also known as  danvi for the best part of a decade now  he's been a very close friend of mine  and a key supporter of my work online he  was there  cheering me on from the very beginning  why do you support my work  so much is cf like covert is a symptom  that you  you lose your sense of taste just at  this point you know a sense of  compulsion that i can't get rid of  you've got stockholm syndrome into  supporting me  [Laughter]  come with me on my run this evening this  is my first time vlogging a run in a  very long time  yep still just a self-conscious also  pixel goes with me  when were you diagnosed with cf i was  diagnosed  shortly after i was born which is when  when the  large majority of cf patients are  diagnosed um i think it was only  a couple of weeks maybe three weeks um  essentially  i was born and i instantly started to  lose weight which obviously is not ideal  for a newborn baby so there were various  sort of tests run  the big one is a sweat test um because  of the  salt channel kind of cause of cf  you have a lot more salt in your sweat  um  and so they can actually use that as a  pretty good baseline of whether someone  has cystic fibrosis or not  we're very lucky to live where we are  and to have views like this to run along  also today was like the hottest day of  the year so far  and uh yeah we had to wait for a while  for the temperature to cool down it's  like 10 o'clock right now  it's like nine it's basically ten are  you recording  yes oh my god it goes  oh wow look at this whoa  wait we're not running this way oh my  gosh for the vlog though  any words we've got to keep moving our  muscles get sore  we've got another 10k to go come on  come on this is my medication shelf  um so this one is a  uh a powder that i inhale morning and  evening it's  called a mucolitic it just helps thin up  the mucus the blue box is an antibiotic  to suppress any infections that are  currently  sort of down there in my lungs i've got  pancreatic enzymes  i've got some vitamins because your body  can't absorb them  as effectively so you've got to take  supplements  holidays are great because you have to  like fly with a letter explaining  why your suitcase is full of pills  how many times would you say you've been  hospitalized with cf  god i i did count at one point  um up until about last year  it was at least two times a year since  the age of about  11 10 maybe usually it's some kind of  lung infection  um that has um got too much to kind of  handle with other  treatments and you have to go in for  intravenous therapy which is  a lot more intense a lot more sort of  actively managed  now gopros don't do very well in the  dark this is like a very well-known  phenomenon  we're about to go into what makes this  one special  oh look at the mist  hang on simon turn on tunnel vision now  it sounds a terribly direct way to  phrase it but how  how long does a person with cystic  fibrosis normally live  so the best way because obviously as i  said it's a spectrum the best way is  looking at average life expectancy  kind of through the years so i was  actually looking at this the other day  back in  1950 um i think the average life  expectancy for  a child with cf was about five and  that's just surely you know because the  treatments just weren't available  as more things sort of came on and we  understood the disease better and  how it affected the body um that  increased so back  in 1990 i think uh  the average life expectancy was in the  20s  as of this year i believe we are  currently somewhere in the high  40s um but  i'm not sure and that will obviously  vary but on a huge range of factors  um but what's important is that yeah it  is  it is a it is a life limiting condition  like ultimately unless  you know i have some horrible accident  it will probably be city fibrosis that  kills me  i wanted to pay back some of the love  and support danvy has shown me over the  years  so this year i'm fundraising for the cf  trust the fact that cf trust exists  means that there is a group of  uh sort of charities and organizations  globally  who can who can and do come together  to share research to share knowledge to  work  on uh global projects to look at  improving the life well-being and  life expectancy of people's cf like in  previous years when i fundraised for a  local cancer hospice and for  conservation charity dural  i'm going to do this via running a half  marathon i'm not a natural runner  having the athletic ability and  proportions of a bowling pin  so this will be a very tough 13.1  miles to run in the past couple of weeks  pixel girl and i actually moved to my  favorite city  in the world bath or she calls it bath  and i'm going to be running the two  tunnels half marathon which is  kind of unique it's the half marathon  with the most distance covered  underground  in the uk you literally run through one  of the big hills surrounding bath  if i'm able to see through the sweat it  should be a really gorgeous route  but hopefully you'll appreciate it in  the vlog that i'll film off the race  the money raised by the cf trust goes to  a few different things that they do a  few different causes  one thing is sort of grants and  financial aid  for the families of those who have  cystic fibrosis because  it does have a financial impact um on  your lives as  any kind of you know long-term  disability does the big one  of course is is research and that can be  as we've mentioned kind of research into  new drugs and new therapies but it can  also be researched  into kind of the associated things like  um mental health impacts on people's cf  and new forms of uh or effects  of different kinds of physiotherapy and  third i guess  um the funding goes to things like  campaigns so either raising awareness or  you know campaigning to to the  government to the nhs  um to to bodies which can help yeah it's  it's a varied a pot and there is so much  what they do and there's so much work  that they want to do that yeah  they know what they're doing essentially  this one is  personal to me i wouldn't be where i am  today and the amazing community around  my videos and my streams wouldn't be  what it is today without  dan and that means i owe a profound debt  of gratitude to the cf  trust if you'd like to help their  life-saving work and help people like  dan live their lives to the fullest  or just donate in advance of what's  hopefully going to be an interesting  vlog  please go to justgiving.com forward  slash  simon clark cf linked in the description  i've been training for a couple of weeks  now and at the time of recording the  half marathon is about a month away  and i'm not feeling terribly confident  about this this is this is going to be  hard  as well as donating if you could please  pray for my legs this is gonna hurt  please donate

